    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "## Key Information\n\n- Most ferrous metals have their maximal strength at about 200 °C (400 °F)\n- Modulus of elasticity decreases gradually with increased temperature until it rapidly falls off\n- The strength of copper is reduced to approximately 95% at 100 °C\n\n## Example - Strength of Copper at 100 °C\n\nWith an Ultimate Tensile Strength (σu) of 220 MPa for copper:\n- Strength reduction at 100 °C: 95%\n- Reduced strength: 0.95 × 220 MPa = 209 MPa"
